" aria-busy=3D"false" dir=3D"ltr" style=3D"--animation-duration: 400ms; --f=
ade-animation-function: linear;"><p data-path-to-node=3D"0">If your dataset=
 climbs from 500K to 50M or 500M, you move from "standard SQL tuning" into =
the realm of <b data-path-to-node=3D"0" data-index-in-node=3D"103">Physical=
 Database Design</b>. When you cross this threshold, the B-Tree indexes tha=
t used to fit in your server's RAM (Buffer Pool) start spilling to disk, an=
d that's when your millisecond latency evaporates.</p><p data-path-to-node=
=3D"1">Here is how partitioning and other advanced strategies keep you in t=
he "fast lane."</p><hr data-path-to-node=3D"2"><h2 data-path-to-node=3D"3">=
How Partitioning Saves the Day</h2><p data-path-to-node=3D"4">Partitioning =
doesn't make a single query faster by magic; it makes the <b data-path-to-n=
ode=3D"4" data-index-in-node=3D"71">working set</b> smaller so the database=
 can find data faster.</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"5">1. Range Partitioning=
 for Idempotency</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"6">For the <code data-path-to-=
node=3D"6" data-index-in-node=3D"8">idempotency_records</code> table, <b da=
ta-path-to-node=3D"6" data-index-in-node=3D"35">Range Partitioning</b> by <=
code data-path-to-node=3D"6" data-index-in-node=3D"57">created_at</code> is=
 a lifesaver.</p><ul data-path-to-node=3D"7"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"7,=
0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"7,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">The Problem:</=
b> Deleting 10 million old rows with <code data-path-to-node=3D"7,0,0" data=
-index-in-node=3D"47">DELETE FROM ... WHERE created_at &lt; ...</code> is a=
 "heavy" operation. It generates massive amounts of WAL (Write Ahead Log) a=
nd causes table bloat.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"7,1,0"><b data-p=
ath-to-node=3D"7,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">The Solution:</b> Create a p=
artition for each day.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"7,2,0"><b data-p=
ath-to-node=3D"7,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">The Benefit:</b> To "clean u=
p" Monday's data on Wednesday, you simply <b data-path-to-node=3D"7,2,0" da=
ta-index-in-node=3D"66">Drop the Partition</b>. This is a metadata operatio=
n that takes microseconds and generates zero bloat.</p></li></ul><h3 data-p=
ath-to-node=3D"8">2. Hash Partitioning for Locks</h3><p data-path-to-node=
=3D"9">For the <code data-path-to-node=3D"9" data-index-in-node=3D"8">resou=
rce_locks</code> table, use <b data-path-to-node=3D"9" data-index-in-node=
=3D"34">Hash Partitioning</b> on <code data-path-to-node=3D"9" data-index-i=
n-node=3D"55">resource_id</code>.</p><ul data-path-to-node=3D"10"><li><p da=
ta-path-to-node=3D"10,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"10,0,0" data-index-in-no=
de=3D"0">The Problem:</b> If 1,000 users are hitting the same index page si=
multaneously, you get <b data-path-to-node=3D"10,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D=
"84">Buffer Contentions</b> (the DB's internal locks on the index itself).<=
/p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"10,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"10,1,0"=
 data-index-in-node=3D"0">The Solution:</b> Split the table into, say, 64 p=
artitions based on a hash of the ID.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"10=
,2,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"10,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">The Benefit:=
</b> Each partition has its own physical file and its own index tree. You e=
ffectively spread the physical I/O and internal latching across 64 differen=
t structures.</p></li></ul><hr data-path-to-node=3D"11"><h2 data-path-to-no=
de=3D"12">If You Still Can't Hit Milliseconds</h2><p data-path-to-node=3D"1=
3">If your database is properly indexed and partitioned but you're still se=
eing 50ms+ latency, you are likely hitting <b data-path-to-node=3D"13" data=
-index-in-node=3D"115">The Three Walls</b>:</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"14"=
>1. The I/O Wall (Disk Wait)</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"15">If your "Activ=
e Data" (the indexes) is larger than your RAM, the DB has to go to the SSD =
for every check.</p><ul data-path-to-node=3D"16"><li><p data-path-to-node=
=3D"16,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"16,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">The Fi=
x:</b> Increase the <code data-path-to-node=3D"16,0,0" data-index-in-node=
=3D"22">buffer_pool_size</code> (MySQL) or <code data-path-to-node=3D"16,0,=
0" data-index-in-node=3D"50">shared_buffers</code> (PostgreSQL). You want y=
our <code data-path-to-node=3D"16,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"93">resource_l=
ocks</code> index to be <b data-path-to-node=3D"16,0,0" data-index-in-node=
=3D"120">100% resident in RAM</b>.</p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"17=
">2. The Connection Wall</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"18">Each time a thread=
 asks for a connection from HikariCP, there is overhead.</p><ul data-path-t=
o-node=3D"19"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"19,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"1=
9,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">The Fix:</b> Ensure your connection pool is=
 sized correctly.</p><div data-path-to-node=3D"19,0,1"><div class=3D"math-b=
lock" data-math=3D"\text{Connections} =3D ((\text{Core Count} \times 2) + \=
text{Effective Spindle Count})"><span class=3D"katex-display"><span class=
