de=3D"0">Note:</i> With Virtual Threads (Java 25), you can have 10,000 thre=
ads but only 50 DB connections. The threads will wait for a connection rath=
er than crashing the DB with 10,000 simultaneous sessions.</p></li></ul><h3=
 data-path-to-node=3D"20">3. The "Unlogged" Strategy</h3><p data-path-to-no=
de=3D"21">In PostgreSQL, you can make the <code data-path-to-node=3D"21" da=
ta-index-in-node=3D"32">resource_locks</code> table <b data-path-to-node=3D=
"21" data-index-in-node=3D"53">UNLOGGED</b>.</p><ul data-path-to-node=3D"22=
"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"22,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"22,0,0" data-=
index-in-node=3D"0">The Trade-off:</b> Data is not written to the WAL. If t=
he database crashes, the table is wiped clean.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-=
node=3D"22,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"22,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Th=
e Benefit:</b> It is significantly faster (up to 3x) because there is no di=
sk write for the log.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"22,2,0"><b data-p=
ath-to-node=3D"22,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Why it works here:</b> Sinc=
e these are just locks, if the DB crashes and everyone loses their locks, t=
hey can just re-acquire them. It=E2=80=99s a "safe" data loss.</p></li></ul=
><hr data-path-to-node=3D"23"><h2 data-path-to-node=3D"24">When to Move Awa=
y from RDBMS</h2><p data-path-to-node=3D"25">If you are doing 100,000+ lock=
 acquisitions <b data-path-to-node=3D"25" data-index-in-node=3D"44">per sec=
ond</b>, an RDBMS (even partitioned) will struggle because of the overhead =
of the ACID transaction engine. At that point, you should move the <code da=
ta-path-to-node=3D"25" data-index-in-node=3D"189">ResourceLock</code> and <=
code data-path-to-node=3D"25" data-index-in-node=3D"206">Idempotency</code>=
 logic to <b data-path-to-node=3D"25" data-index-in-node=3D"227">Redis</b>.=
</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"26">Why Redis?</h3><ul data-path-to-node=3D"27=
"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"27,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"27,0,0" data-=
index-in-node=3D"0">In-Memory by Design:</b> Sub-millisecond latency is the=
 default.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"27,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=
=3D"27,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Lua Scripting:</b> You can write an "A=
tomic Acquire" script in Lua that runs inside Redis, providing the same "At=
omic SQL" logic we wrote but in RAM.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"27=
,2,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"27,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Redlock:</b>=
 Redis has its own proven distributed lock algorithm.</p></li></ul><hr data=
-path-to-node=3D"28"><h2 data-path-to-node=3D"29">Summary: The Scaling Path=
</h2><ol start=3D"1" data-path-to-node=3D"30"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"3=
0,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"30,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">500K Entiti=
es:</b> Standard Indexes (You are here).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=
=3D"30,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"30,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">5M Ent=
ities:</b> Tune Buffer Pools / Increase RAM.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-no=
de=3D"30,2,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"30,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">50M =
Entities:</b> Implement Range/Hash Partitioning.</p></li><li><p data-path-t=
o-node=3D"30,3,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"30,3,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">=
500M Entities / 100k TPS:</b> Move the Lock Engine to Redis or etcd, but ke=
ep the <code data-path-to-node=3D"30,3,0" data-index-in-node=3D"78">Documen=
t</code> data in the RDBMS.</p></li></ol><p data-path-to-node=3D"31"><b dat=
a-path-to-node=3D"31" data-index-in-node=3D"0">The "Millisecond Test":</b> =
Run <code data-path-to-node=3D"31" data-index-in-node=3D"28">EXPLAIN ANALYZ=
E</code> on your lock acquisition query. If the "Index Scan" time is low bu=
t "Execution Time" is high, you have a lock contention problem. If "Index S=
can" is high, you have a memory/disk problem.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"32=
">Which of those metrics are you most concerned about for your week-long te=
st?</p></div></message-content><!----></div></structured-content-container>=
